#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167722} :activity/id #uuid "775b7c3e-3e96-5ea8-a95f-883523a63b42" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423 :topic/id #uuid "f3e0d484-510c-5c15-beb2-bef21881c55b" :topic/math-academy-id 3235} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167722 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168109 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis with acceleration $a(t),$ velocity $v(t)$ and displacement $x(t),$ where $t$ is the time. If we want to calculate the acceleration of $P,$ we use the relationship \n\n$$\na(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d t}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we know the acceleration and we want to find the velocity, we use the relationship\n\n$$\nv(t) = \\int a(t)\\,\\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nIn both of these relationships, we require $a$ and $v$ to be expressed as functions of $t.$ \n\nNow suppose that we're given the acceleration as a function of the displacement $x.$ How do we calculate the velocity in this case?\n\nFor example, suppose that the acceleration of a particle is given by $a(x) = 4x\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$ A sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](images/01/017463f3ac8abac9c2b00c4d9a715a4109a5f4bc1b070e0a12c4d3a7bac64351.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ equals $4x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}t} = 4x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite the left-hand side of this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}$ by definition, we have\n\n$$\nv \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}v}{\\textrm{d}x} = 4x.\\qquad \\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nAlso, by the chain rule for differentiation, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}(v^2) = 2v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = v\\dfrac{\\textrm d v}{\\textrm d x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the differential equation $(\\ast)$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d }{\\textrm d x}\\left(\\dfrac12v^2\\right) = 4x.\n$$\n\nBy solving this differential equation, we can find an expression for the velocity $v$ in terms of displacement $x.$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "291c6fee-a1ab-554d-b0b8-5508bd23eb2e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5369 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "af56a6fb-d731-5179-b8b7-de268aeb13f2" :step/math-academy-id 33520 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167727}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167727 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166853 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166705 :question/id #uuid "e8353dba-48d7-5f4f-8ba5-97e4e22b6e44" :question/math-academy-id "e-9050"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25214773-e6cb-5663-95fe-a04408844902" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256602} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256606} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378470}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "ea23ed7c-763e-54fa-af53-bdcd85190355" :step/math-academy-id 33782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167728}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167728 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166706 :question/id #uuid "49e02115-6211-564b-8221-d5835a98a064" :question/math-academy-id "e-9051"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382971}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle"} :step/id #uuid "87d6c80e-5ac1-5210-accd-0df7f165185b" :step/math-academy-id 33783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166862 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166715 :question/id #uuid "80b653d0-5dff-5621-86ee-e1fd336ad740" :question/math-academy-id "e-9455"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048282}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167089} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256603} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256605} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256610} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400058}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time"} :step/id #uuid "2a283e70-969e-5e03-8f63-5c695f48086d" :step/math-academy-id 35398}] :activity/title "Velocity and Acceleration as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167007}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420431} :activity/id #uuid "058e9265-ff9b-5baf-8f41-85e7c7868e51" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423 :topic/id #uuid "f3e0d484-510c-5c15-beb2-bef21881c55b" :topic/math-academy-id 3235} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256625 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39825938-ad5f-5161-82be-a58bd1817a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254173} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee5c0c6-750e-5668-bdc0-064e5c9b4f07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254925} {:answer/id #uuid "a5170868-e27c-5ec2-bb54-083bfe57da02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=2{e}^{-2t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255126} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd96b2d-0804-5239-be68-eadc7bae04bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} {:answer/id #uuid "e85d341b-3675-5698-9153-635f1bc811e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255629} :answer-field/id #uuid "795c6c90-3b12-5b40-b826-7991e61fdee3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ac389cb5-50b0-5d9d-9387-c7be6e7524a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-122352" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at $x=1\\,m$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f08c9d1e8dce3c0ae7c37719cadc3fc60bf5de3030ce8c44ca7528a98f3abf7e.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $8{e}^{1-x}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-8{e}^{1-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -8{e}^{1-x}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=4\\,m/s$ when $x=1\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8{e}^{0}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8{e}^{1-x} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16{e}^{1-x} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{e}^{(1-x)/2}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4{e}^{(1-x)/2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{e}^{(1-x)/2}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int {e}^{(x-1)/2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ 2{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ {e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{{C}_{1}}{2}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=1$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{e}^{(x-1)/2}=2t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{(x-1)/2} & =2t+1 \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x-1 & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1) \\\\ x & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1)+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03c63cb6-dfb8-5730-962f-2e6452481748"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016a3368-f9d5-58aa-ab05-aad63edb1b18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253680} {:answer/id #uuid "1e97ff28-e0bb-54b7-b8b3-9417ec6fd261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} {:answer/id #uuid "2371f7e7-c2e4-57e1-bd77-d71c072ed039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+x+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253988} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5e00e1-832f-5f55-83f9-b8c48153a1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255339} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf76d94-8303-5033-a055-32483b709927" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)={x}^{2}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255884}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253929} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a51decc-a151-5408-9864-1176626e3665" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4bd8ece4-afaf-5640-9ad2-c2e8ef66ae37" :question/math-academy-id "q-122338" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/98/983beaf02582c5ebaee129e28bba657eb2db5e4da7f74eb4b8eba73b9083a259.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(2x+1)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2x+1.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={x}^{2}+x+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={1}^{2}+1+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+x+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{2({x}^{2}+x+6)}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b4469a6-0f97-5a60-8a7e-bd0dcb2520b2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420431 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256608 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a314d43-645b-5c09-84cc-babb825962d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253749} {:answer/id #uuid "399960c6-8915-55cf-b499-430d9554395e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9f49c1-1e43-5f1b-aef8-2b18efec441e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255187} {:answer/id #uuid "c08c3b51-5261-57f3-8880-d02902e00fe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255385} {:answer/id #uuid "cbeb2a0e-c3b9-59b0-8c51-5cca3ce4d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3})=2{x}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255481}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254174} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae42a556-6e64-58a1-9826-bc4a1d962a7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f3eb3e3-8dee-5051-9c40-8fe59ac84fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-122319" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b2/b23ef6e4df05874a715271c578dfa57d5ddacabef2d0801edc4e736c7de66e17.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $2{x}^{4}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=2{x}^{4}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({v}^{2})=2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x},\n$$\n\nwe can write our differential equation $(∗)$ as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=2{x}^{4}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "798abf0e-ceda-5e61-8354-4bf0a2e66635" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420432 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4364ff09-2922-52f1-bb5f-e67a3ba34a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} {:answer/id #uuid "5b2164e7-b48b-5848-83ea-558ab8d57139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{2t+1}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254476} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc028c3-519a-5570-a621-cd76222813f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(12t-1)^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254492} {:answer/id #uuid "da22387e-a7bf-5ddb-9d10-96871c121ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\sqrt[3]{t-8}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255605} {:answer/id #uuid "fd4a3bfe-c5a0-5f5f-b52c-5d5d734cceb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=(3t+2)^{3}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255897}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186254246} :answer-field/id #uuid "f893dcb4-30ee-50df-9369-932bd543c3cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ec791f70-e6b1-5d28-9a5c-0e20d98d3307" :question/math-academy-id "q-122349" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed toward $O.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=1\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/14/141c6dd6c91841238298b86fac1063c76faeb7cd103e13d2723f6ac05ed58f50.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -\\frac{32}{(x+2)^{5}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(0+2)^{4}}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{(x+2)^{4}} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{4}{(x+2)^{2}} \\\\ (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int (x+2)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}(x+2)^{3} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+2)^{3} & =12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=3{C}_{1}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=8,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{3}=12t+8.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)^{3} & =12t+8 \\\\ x+2 & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{12t+8}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a1a065d0-24c6-5622-ba75-1f337d376e40" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256616 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e5f7fd-5f39-54d0-b24b-d6386ba4b120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{3-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254422} {:answer/id #uuid "7715943a-4b29-5731-aa1b-21b6a9f5bcda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)={2}^{1-x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254725} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7f8494-68d3-5ece-b55e-5a21d83663af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254759} {:answer/id #uuid "bbaa6296-4ac5-5e74-907b-e485c987c549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa68c5c-78bd-533d-9a2b-0493c8ac2f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v(x)=\\frac{4}{3}\\sqrt{{2}^{3-x}+5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255345} :answer-field/id #uuid "6837870e-c4ce-53d3-8d92-89b9b7c8217b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9efcc68b-3929-5bf9-ab56-4c19b9a57740" :question/math-academy-id "q-122341" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $1\\,m$ when its velocity is $4\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/6d/6ddbfef68a0b41a6ee3f11c46ff7821f7ff0dc23a16b4244b5e7a1f6579d7dd2.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude ${2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (-{2}^{2-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={2}^{2-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=1\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & ={2}^{2-1}+C \\\\ 8 & =2+C \\\\ C & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={2}^{2-x}+6 \\\\ {v}^{2} & ={2}^{3-x}+12 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4({2}^{1-x}+3) \\\\ v & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=2\\sqrt{{2}^{1-x}+3}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c5db5cd4-2409-5a4a-be21-19989f057f9b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420432}}] :activity/title "Velocity and Acceleration as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420428}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437149} :activity/id #uuid "feee590a-367b-57bd-af52-b43897192919" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049423 :topic/id #uuid "f3e0d484-510c-5c15-beb2-bef21881c55b" :topic/math-academy-id 3235} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8658c0bb-cfd4-5142-bbcb-e4097359b029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.7\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254860} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc47ff9-abd8-5a14-858a-de531e040383" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255056} {:answer/id #uuid "b5ad6d9c-592b-5e0c-8122-d62c500c3d21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255273} {:answer/id #uuid "b9989186-dace-584b-b203-97a637274263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3.1\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0cc1f7-7a63-5b1c-a0d5-346590852e30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255630}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255322} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd66982-5b26-55ef-acff-14d539285859" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8d544f0e-e224-5a8f-b2d9-7d23deb9a689" :question/math-academy-id "q-122347" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ the magnitude of its acceleration is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and the acceleration is directed toward $O.$ If $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/3c/3c5450b18c5dc58b082faadf4086b6a21c0c9b55afb016090147609a2ccfe719.png)\n\nSince the magnitude of the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\,{m/s}^{2}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int [-{3}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}={3}^{1-x}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=-2\\sqrt{2}\\,m/s$ when $x=2\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(-2\\sqrt{2})^{2} & ={3}^{1-2}+C \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{11}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & ={3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3}) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-x}+\\frac{11}{3})}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =-\\sqrt{2({3}^{1-1}+\\frac{11}{3})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{28}{3}} \\\\  & ≈-3.1\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} :step/id #uuid "a96f26a1-150f-53aa-92db-8203a5d7c383"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5b2eeb-074d-51bf-9b3f-10803e274c41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253770} {:answer/id #uuid "56351265-a52c-5206-885d-fa5dfeb276eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254428} {:answer/id #uuid "afa87942-4e3f-5a55-ae5f-5d22dd94cb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255214} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b91cfd-f727-572e-865c-a94f7aba262d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{v}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255559} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bf1029-4403-54dd-a55a-c9e8275e658a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255702} :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d4fe-7d5a-50cb-99f3-481a645a95f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a2aa7557-417c-5f06-bca6-b63a94c74ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-122307" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and the magnitude of its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/47/473a3f91d91351499d5849ee07a86cfcfc2b257949d6ebba68b3261201f2bf68.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d63723b3-39af-5ef2-8a04-28cdc6c2794d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437148}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437148 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08989d71-d44c-554a-bce5-f49fba344fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253732} {:answer/id #uuid "485c6662-97ec-5254-8bb7-1d15dbc744b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254291} {:answer/id #uuid "d23b0337-7b09-5e9a-8cfa-fce6700a9517" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} {:answer/id #uuid "eac5aa36-49a3-5648-998f-1f48c9122a73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255726} {:answer/id #uuid "edac6096-b0c5-5959-8d95-58a0e84227a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255544} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cd69269-dfc3-5489-a6f7-c96d7e0ca756" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256068}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "226fe9ff-a963-5e5f-900b-474a261bf3ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122310" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dac6231b-7098-5146-a02c-d7abae0838f2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437150}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167089 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ebf18ee-af52-58eb-9db4-f851658ecd7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253931} {:answer/id #uuid "1f068fc2-f6b1-5e9e-af2b-751b98bcdc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253936} {:answer/id #uuid "49f209c9-1626-5df5-aefb-bdf07e8a5715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=\\frac{1}{2}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254305} {:answer/id #uuid "de5cdbb3-0dd8-544a-8444-7a6932630c5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)=1-{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255642} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e425f-e29f-5937-be75-0ebb5ff34e95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x(t)={e}^{t}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186255683} :answer-field/id #uuid "877a8eaf-76c0-5b50-a12d-6f6b23536812" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8488fc53-7024-5b0e-acc8-a7217b9f9e03" :question/math-academy-id "q-122170" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, the velocity is given by $v(x)=(1+x)\\,m/s.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a8/a8a6f49ead700bee856b1bbdbb10848eac0a57609da14e3967916a36eb56f048.png)\n\nWe're given that the velocity function $v(x)$ is\n\n$$\nv(x)=1+x.\n$$\n\nWe know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1+x.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1+x \\\\ \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{1+x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+x| & =t+K \\\\ 1+x & =C{e}^{t} \\\\ x & =C{e}^{t}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={e}^{t}-1.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f9140de0-2c2b-50c8-a7e7-ea66ba1fa4cb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437147}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437150 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256617 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032c1aed-5869-5996-b874-ec60376b4b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.8\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} {:answer/id #uuid "3c64d957-8cab-5c68-a933-ebb3da3c2a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254193} {:answer/id #uuid "484acfee-7a2b-52bf-a78b-d62f355b27df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254288} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5dc97-9ff5-56d3-816d-361c1ad73349" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.3\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255472} {:answer/id #uuid "d91174a7-6095-546d-814c-2cd0e4c8e550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253693} :answer-field/id #uuid "0641e0bb-3057-51af-9319-471e1e0b8f5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8a56f0b7-8350-56d0-8d3b-b2d932d38f5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-122343" :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x,$ away from a fixed origin $O.$ When the displacement of $P$ from $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ find the velocity of $P$ when $x=1\\,\\text{m}.$ Round your answer to one decimal place." :question/worked-solution "![](images/0d/0dd98b517e2ed800f12a3672d2d6f45021f7f502cfe98c1e9308e52c05ab5f38.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is ${e}^{x/2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})={e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=2{e}^{x/2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since  $v=\\sqrt{5}\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,\\text{m},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{5})^{2} & =2{e}^{0/2}+C \\\\ \\frac{5}{2} & =2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =2{e}^{x/2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =4{e}^{x/2}+1 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv=\\sqrt{4{e}^{x/2}+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity of $P$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\sqrt{4{e}^{1/2}+1} \\\\ v & =\\sqrt{4\\sqrt{e}+1} \\\\  & ≈2.8\\,\\text{m/s},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."} :step/id #uuid "f9498a14-80ad-5194-bc2c-7090bc99b1db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437146}}] :activity/title "Velocity and Acceleration as Functions of Displacement" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437145}]}
